\begin{abstract}
Large language models are increasingly used as annotators in computational social science, yet annotation errors propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the confusion matrix structure and the causal DAG topology. We derive bias expressions for seven canonical topologies under $K$-class non-differential misclassification and show that $K {\geq} 3$ introduces a structural possibility of sign reversal absent in the binary case. We prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, and verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door; exposure and IV topologies can reverse the treatment effect sign. Under realistic LLM accuracy (diagonal ${\geq}\,0.7$), the sign reversal rate is 2.1\%, exclusively in secondary coefficients---in all 23 empirical configurations tested, the primary treatment effect does not reverse---consistent with the attenuation structure when category effects share the same sign. We introduce Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs) revealing a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile) to M-bias (immune), and show that matrix-based corrections are provably ineffective for four topologies due to Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance. Topology-aware analysis is necessary for valid causal inference with LLM-generated annotations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We validate the bias taxonomy and ASV framework through analytical derivations and Monte Carlo simulation across seven DAG topologies under $K{=}3$ non-differential misclassification.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

For each of the seven topologies (confounding, exposure, mediation, collider, M-bias, front-door, IV), we sample 100{,}000 confusion matrices from a Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, compute the analytical probability limit of the causal estimator, and validate against Monte Carlo simulation ($N{=}10{,}000$). ASV experiments use $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$.

\subsection{Bias Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy_results}

\paragraph{Theory-MC agreement.}
Analytical probability limits match Monte Carlo estimates with grand MAPE of 2.0\% across 126 scenarios (18 LLM configurations $\times$ 7 topologies, $N{=}10{,}000$). Per-topology MAPE ranges from 0.5\% (confounding) to 5.5\% (IV); the IV maximum reflects the Wald estimator's small-denominator variance amplification.

\paragraph{Exposure DAG: sign reversal under multi-class misclassification.}
Under the full Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, 85\% of $K{=}3$ confusion matrices produce bias that violates the binary ``toward-null'' guarantee. More critically, 83.4\% of matrices cause \emph{sign reversal} of at least one coefficient. The two failure modes are distinct: for a coefficient with true value $\beta_1{=}1.0$, a near-permutation $C$ produces $\hat{\gamma}_1{=}{-}0.84$ (sign reversal, magnitude $0.84{\times}$); for $\beta_2{=}{-}0.5$, the same $C$ yields $\hat{\gamma}_2{=}{-}1.41$ (same-sign amplification, $2.83{\times}$).

This finding persists in practically relevant subspaces. When restricting to confusion matrices with diagonal entries $\geq 0.7$ (typical of current LLM classifiers achieving $F_1 > 0.8$), the violation rate remains 13.7\% with 2.1\% sign reversal and 11.7\% amplification. Even at diagonal $\geq 0.9$, violation persists at 11.9\%. In contrast, the $K{=}2$ control confirms that all six tested binary confusion matrices maintain the classical bias-toward-null guarantee~\cite{brenner1993}, establishing that the multi-class failure is structurally distinct from the binary case.

\paragraph{Structural guarantee for non-exposure topologies.}
Five of seven topologies exhibit zero sign-flip rate across all sampled matrices (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). Confounding, mediation, and front-door show 100\% violation and amplification but never reverse the sign.\footnote{Consistent with \citet{brenner1993} and \citet{dosemeci1990}: polytomous misclassification can bias \emph{away} from the null (amplification), but our sign-flip metric confirms the effect sign is preserved.} Collider misclassification is protective: conditioning on noisy $A^*$ induces \emph{less} collider bias than conditioning on $A$ ($|B(C)|$ approximately non-increasing; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}). M-bias shows the weakest sensitivity (53.2\% violation, 10.4\% amplification).

\paragraph{IV: Wald estimator instability.}
The IV topology is the sole non-exposure exception: 52.6\% of random matrices cause the Wald estimator's denominator to change sign. This rate is stable across $K{=}3,5,7$, confirming structural fragility. Unlike exposure, IV violation remains at 100\% even at diagonal $\geq 0.9$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}).

All structural patterns persist at $K{=}5,7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_k_robustness}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Bias characteristics of $K{=}3$ non-differential misclassification across 7 DAG topologies. Violation: $|\hat{\tau}| > |\tau|$; Sign flip: $\text{sign}(\hat{\tau}) \neq \text{sign}(\tau)$; Amplification: $|\hat{\tau}| > |\tau|$ with same sign; $\tau_{\text{sf}}{=}0$?: sign-flip guarantee holds unconditionally (\checkmark), under DGP-specific sufficient conditions (\checkmark$^*$; see Proposition~\ref{prop:signflip} and Numerical Verification~1), or does not hold (\ding{55}). Rates over 100{,}000 Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ random confusion matrices.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Topology} & \textbf{Violation} & \textbf{Sign Flip} & \textbf{Amplif.} & $\tau_{\text{sf}}{=}0$? \\
\midrule
Confounding & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
Exposure & 85\% & 83.4\% & 14.1\% & \ding{55} \\
Mediation & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
Front-door & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
Collider & 100\% & 0\% & 100\% & \checkmark$^*$ \\
M-bias & 53.2\% & 0\% & 10.4\% & \checkmark \\
IV & 100\% & 52.6\% & 100\% & \ding{55} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The bias distributions are visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:bias_distribution}.

\subsection{ASV Framework Validation}
\label{sec:asv_results}

\paragraph{Envelope monotonicity.}
The ASV envelope (bias magnitude as a function of $\delta$) is $\varepsilon$-approximately monotonic for non-IV topologies: collider and M-bias achieve $\varepsilon < 0.03$ across all tested configurations; front-door achieves $\varepsilon < 0.06$ for moderate $\tau$; exposure satisfies $\varepsilon_{p99} < 0.008$ (Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}; Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify}). IV is excluded: the Wald estimator exhibits singularities when the first-stage coefficient passes through zero, producing unbounded deviations. These bounded deviations for non-IV topologies confirm that $\delta$ serves as a meaningful ``dial'' controlling annotation error severity.

\paragraph{Magnitude ASV.}
Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid} reports magnitude ASV ranges at $\tau{=}0.5$ with a 10\% bias threshold, ranking from most fragile to most robust: front-door (0.064--0.387) $<$ mediation $<$ confounding $<$ IV (0.101--0.607) $<$ collider (0.338--2.028) $<$ M-bias ($\infty$). M-bias achieves infinite ASV because annotation error only reduces the already-small spurious path.

\paragraph{Significance and sign-flip ASV.}
At $\tau{=}0.05$, significance ASV achieves 100\% power for confounding, front-door, and M-bias, but 0\% for mediation, collider, and IV (correct behavior for attenuating topologies; Type~I error is 0\%). For sign-flip ASV, 83.4\% of exposure reference matrices yield $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} < 1$ (extreme sensitivity); IV sign-flip ASV ranges 1.1--6.7 (moderate-to-high). The complete ranking (Figure~\ref{fig:asv_ranking}, Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}) confirms IV as maximally fragile and M-bias as immune (ASV $= \infty$).

\paragraph{$N$-sensitivity.}
Significance ASV exhibits a sharp phase transition at a critical $N^* \approx 6{,}000$--$10{,}000$ (analytically computable per topology), below which all rejections are blocked. Bootstrap CIs ($B{=}1{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{sec:bootstrap_asv}): $\delta^* = 0.94\,[0.60, 1.36]$ (exposure), $0.75\,[0.44, 1.29]$ (IV).

\subsection{Empirical Validation}
\label{sec:empirical}

We validate using real LLM confusion matrices from three tasks: HumAID~\cite{alam2021humaid} ($K{=}10$), VAST ($K{=}3$), and CivilComments ($K{=}2$). Four LLMs (Llama-4-Maverick, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-Chat, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) produce 23 configurations across the three datasets under zero-shot and few-shot prompting, yielding 161 bias scenarios ($23 \times 7$ topologies).

\paragraph{HumAID ($K{=}10$): theory--practice alignment.}
The empirical bias ranking matches predictions: IV (12.4--42.1\%), exposure (9.0--17.4\%), mediation/front-door (10.7--14.8\%), while collider, confounding, and M-bias remain below 10\%---consistent with the ASV vulnerability spectrum.

\paragraph{Cross-$K$ comparison.}
Bias varies substantially across settings: VAST ($K{=}3$) produces 62.8\% mean bias with IV reaching 326\%, versus 12.2\% for HumAID ($K{=}10$) and 32.4\% for CivilComments ($K{=}2$). Even CivilComments configurations with $F_1{=}0.82$ (Qwen3-235B) produce 62--92\% IV bias, confirming that classification accuracy does not predict downstream causal risk (Figure~\ref{fig:f1_vs_bias}).\footnote{VAST's near-degenerate confusion matrices (Neutral-class recall 3.6--16.1\%) drive its extreme IV bias via the Wald estimator's small-denominator amplification.}

Checking all $K{-}1$ exposure coefficients, 14 of 23 configurations (61\%) exhibit sign reversal in at least one coefficient---all in the exposure topology. The primary coefficient ($\beta_1$, largest magnitude) never reverses; sign flips occur exclusively in secondary coefficients with small $|\beta|$ corresponding to rare categories. Sign reversal requires opposite-sign treatment effects across categories (Equation~\ref{eq:exposure}); same-sign $\beta_k$ produce only attenuation. Zero sign reversals occur across 138 non-exposure scenarios ($23 {\times} 6$ topologies), validating the structural guarantee $K$-invariantly.

ASV diagnostics flag 55.4\% of HumAID and 100\% of CivilComments scenarios as magnitude-unsafe despite $F_1$ up to 0.82 (Appendix~\ref{sec:asv_diagnostics}). Sign-flip patterns are preserved under logistic DGP ($|\Delta\,\text{SF}_\tau| < 0.02$; Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_f1_vs_bias.pdf}
\caption{Topology determines downstream causal bias, not $F_1$. HumAID $K{=}10$ (56 points): within $F_1$ range 0.614--0.690, bias varies $10{\times}$ (IV 42.1\% vs.\ collider $<$5.6\%).}
\label{fig:f1_vs_bias}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods (naive plug-in, MLA, DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023}) across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations; full results in Table~\ref{tab:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
For four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias), FWL implies that any linear transformation of the dummy matrix $\mathbf{D}^*$ ($C^{-1}$ premultiplication, MLA, SIMEX) preserves the column space relative to $T$, leaving the treatment coefficient unchanged (Appendix~\ref{app:fwl_proof}). Naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction.

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean RMSE reduction (47.0\%) but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%). MC-SIMEX provides gold-free correction (best for IV at 59.4\%) but harms front-door ($-$62.4\%). For protective topologies, correction re-exposes the original bias pathway. No single method dominates: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:case_study}).

